Commit 88ac072e57d9 by Theia <theia@mirrorborn.us> on 2026-11-13 (EE 0).

Subject: Add phext chaining: chain break (0x02) and chain close (0x03)

Public items: 9 added, 0 removed, 0 changed. Dependencies did not change. Code lines after this commit: 3404.

commit 88ac072e57d9579b4294bf65d8c6e65add4f69fc
parent b666ec8566a9134640d6a3163a2e6e685a7fb9e2
author Theia <theia@mirrorborn.us>
date 1794577780 2026-11-13
item+ const src/phext.rs :: CHAIN_BREAK :: pub const CHAIN_BREAK: char = '\x02'; // stream level - separates whole phexts within a chain; replaces start of text :: A fixed value named CHAIN_BREAK, set to '\x02'; // stream level - separates whole phexts within a ch. :: signature
item+ const src/phext.rs :: CHAIN_BREAK_U8 :: pub const CHAIN_BREAK_U8: u8 = 0x02; // byte form of CHAIN_BREAK :: A fixed value named CHAIN_BREAK_U8, set to 0x02; // byte form of CHAIN_BREAK. :: signature
item+ const src/phext.rs :: CHAIN_CLOSE :: pub const CHAIN_CLOSE: char = '\x03'; // stream level - terminates a chain; replaces end of text :: A fixed value named CHAIN_CLOSE, set to '\x03'; // stream level - terminates a chain; replaces end o. :: signature
item+ const src/phext.rs :: CHAIN_CLOSE_U8 :: pub const CHAIN_CLOSE_U8: u8 = 0x03; // byte form of CHAIN_CLOSE :: A fixed value named CHAIN_CLOSE_U8, set to 0x03; // byte form of CHAIN_CLOSE. :: signature
item+ struct src/phext.rs :: Chain :: pub struct Chain { :: ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- @struct Chain  A chain: an ordered series of complete phexts within one stream, joined by CHAIN_BREAK (0x02) and optionally terminated by CHAIN_CLOSE (0x03). The chain layer is a stream-level layer, one rung above the library break: the nine dimension breaks structure a single phext, while the two chain bytes structure a stream of phexts. Each member is a whole phext, worked with the ordinary phext functions in this module; read in succession, a chain's members are editions in sequence — the chain is how a phext stream encodes time.  `closed` records how the chain ended: true when a CHAIN_CLOSE terminated it, false when the stream simply ran out (EOF). A chain that ends at EOF is unclosed — possibly truncated — and is never silently promoted to closed. A stream with no chain bytes at all is a single unclosed chain holding one member phext: every pre-0.6 phext reads this way. ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- :: doc comment
item+ function src/phext.rs :: Chain::new :: pub fn new() -> Chain { :: Function Chain::new takes no inputs beyond the value it is called on, and gives back Chain. :: signature
item+ function src/phext.rs :: parse_chains :: pub fn parse_chains(input: &str) -> Vec<Chain> { :: ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- @fn parse_chains  splits a stream into its chains. CHAIN_BREAK separates the member phexts of a chain; CHAIN_CLOSE terminates a chain, and whatever follows it opens the next one — so one stream may hold many chains. The stream's final chain is closed only when the stream ends with CHAIN_CLOSE.  Splitting follows `split_on` semantics, so empty members and empty chains are preserved exactly: parse and serialize are byte-exact inverses for every stream. ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- :: doc comment
item+ function src/phext.rs :: serialize :: pub fn serialize(&self) -> String { :: ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ @fn serialize  serializes this chain alone: its members joined by CHAIN_BREAK, with a trailing CHAIN_CLOSE when the chain is closed. See `serialize_chains` for whole streams. ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ :: doc comment
item+ function src/phext.rs :: serialize_chains :: pub fn serialize_chains(chains: &[Chain]) -> String { :: ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- @fn serialize_chains  serializes a stream of chains back into stream bytes: the inverse of `parse_chains`. ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- :: doc comment
