Subject: Add borrowed span index: spans() + ScrollSpan over a &str

Public items: 2 added, 0 removed, 1 changed. Dependencies did not change. Code lines after this commit: 6852.

commit cf57de8b7520d40a2da1d3ede842886120aa785c
parent 62a825ef4f8c9bea229a4e1c0301bbc9e4d733e5
author Emise <emise@localhost>
date 1794978119 2026-11-18
item+ struct src/phext.rs :: ScrollSpan :: pub struct ScrollSpan { :: ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- @struct ScrollSpan  The borrowed form of a `PositionedScroll`: a scroll's coordinate plus the byte range of its text within the input document. Slicing the input with `range` yields exactly the scroll's text — building the index allocates no `String` per scroll. ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- :: doc comment
item~ function src/phext.rs :: append :: pub fn append(input: String, location: Coordinate, scroll: &str) -> Option<String> { :: ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- @fn append  Appends `scroll` at `location`, which must sort strictly after the document's last non-empty scroll. The result is the input with only a delimiter run and the new content added at its end — a host can write just that suffix to a file opened in append mode and sync once.  Returns None when the scroll is empty (empty scrolls do not exist) or `location` does not sort after the document's end. Trailing delimiter bytes in the input encode only empty scrolls, so they are dropped first and never shift the appended scroll's address.  On None the append was rejected; the input String was consumed, so pass a clone if the caller needs it after a rejected append. ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- :: doc comment
item+ function src/phext.rs :: spans :: pub fn spans(input: &str) -> Vec<ScrollSpan> { :: ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- @fn spans  Builds a borrowed span index over `input`: one `ScrollSpan` per scroll, in document order, giving each scroll's coordinate and the byte range of its text in `input`.  The walk is the `phokenize` walk — the same dimension breaks and the same coordinate arithmetic — except that it records byte ranges instead of allocating a `String` for every scroll. For every returned span, `&input[span.range]` is exactly the scroll `explode` returns at `span.coord`, and `span.range.start` is the offset `offset` reports for that coordinate.  Empty-scroll contract: a scroll exists iff it holds at least one byte — the definition `explode` encodes — so `spans` reports only non-empty scrolls. Delimiter runs that encode empty scrolls (leading breaks, runs between distant scrolls, trailing breaks) produce no span; an empty document, or a document of only delimiters, produces no spans. `phokenize` remains the tokenizer of record for empty slots: it additionally reports a final empty positioned scroll in those trailing cases, where `spans` reports none.  Chain bytes (0x02 / 0x03) are not dimension breaks, so — exactly as in `phokenize` and `explode` — they are scroll content here. This index covers a single phext (one universe); chain structure is the `parse_chains` layer above it.  Motivating consumer: holo-phext, whose `src/index.rs` carries a 323-line re-implementation of this walk, plus a duplicated private dimension-break predicate in its core, because libphext exported only owned-`String` tokenization (`phokenize`/`explode`) and a single-scroll `offset`. Downstream stores should index with `spans` instead of re-walking the format.  # Example  ``` use libphext::phext::{spans, to_coordinate}; let input = "alpha\u{17}beta"; let index = spans(input); assert_eq!(index.len(), 2); assert_eq!(index[0].coord, to_coordinate("1.1.1/1.1.1/1.1.1")); assert_eq!(&input[index[0].range.clone()], "alpha"); assert_eq!(&input[index[1].range.clone()], "beta"); ``` ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- :: doc comment
