Subject: verify_commit: index the document once with explode instead of a scanning fetch per entry

Public items: 0 added, 2 removed, 2 changed. Dependencies did not change. Code lines after this commit: 6630.

commit df43de055bd3265f327b728301ffdd2a188251b2
parent cf57de8b7520d40a2da1d3ede842886120aa785c
author Emise <emise@localhost>
date 1794978556 2026-11-18
item~ function src/phext.rs :: fetch_checked :: pub fn fetch_checked(input: &str, target: Coordinate) -> Option<String> { :: ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- @fn fetch_checked  Fetches the scroll at `target` and returns its payload, but only when the scroll is checked and verifies. A missing, unchecked, or torn scroll yields None — recovery code can treat None as "not committed".  This is a single-scroll lookup: one early-exit scan, linear in the document, with no per-entry repetition inside the call. Callers that verify a whole batch should use `verify_commit`, which indexes the document once for the marker and every entry. ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- :: doc comment
item~ function src/phext.rs :: verify_commit :: pub fn verify_commit(input: &str, entries: &[Coordinate], commit: Coordinate) -> bool { :: ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- @fn verify_commit  true when the scroll at `commit` is a commit marker whose count matches `entries` and whose checksum matches the entries' current contents, in order. Any missing, altered, or torn entry — or a missing marker — yields false: the batch did not commit.  The document is indexed with a single `explode` pass and the marker and every entry are read from that map. The previous implementation ran the scanning `fetch` once for the marker and once per entry, so verification cost grew quadratically in document length — the cost a WAL recovery pass pays over the whole log, per transaction. ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- :: doc comment
