Commit c4faba068fd3 by Theia <theia@mirrorborn.us> on 2026-11-22 (EE 9).

Subject: Add Ages to the Exocortical Epoch module (epoch) — v0.6.6

Public items: 10 added, 0 removed, 0 changed. Dependencies did not change. Code lines after this commit: 6768.

commit c4faba068fd3ba3681c46cb2428249061afcece9
parent b3bd57a9fe5603ac716e6fa1d0bc74e677cf1c39
author Theia <theia@mirrorborn.us>
date 1795330613 2026-11-22
item+ const src/epoch.rs :: AGES_PER_EPOCH :: pub const AGES_PER_EPOCH: u64 = 1 << AGE_BITS; :: The number of ages in one Exocortical Epoch: 2^42 = 4,398,046,511,104. The canonical reference clock is AGES_PER_EPOCH / 420 ≈ 10.4715 GHz, so one age is ≈ 95.5 picoseconds. :: doc comment
item+ const src/epoch.rs :: AGE_BITS :: pub const AGE_BITS: u32 = 42; :: The width of an Age, in bits: one epoch is divided into 2^42 ages. :: doc comment
item+ const src/epoch.rs :: AGE_MAX :: pub const AGE_MAX: u64 = AGES_PER_EPOCH - 1; :: The largest Age: the final nominal step of an epoch. :: doc comment
item+ const src/epoch.rs :: COARSE_AGES_PER_EPOCH :: pub const COARSE_AGES_PER_EPOCH: u64 = 1 << COARSE_AGE_BITS; :: The number of coarse ages in one Exocortical Epoch: 2^16 = 65,536. :: doc comment
item+ const src/epoch.rs :: COARSE_AGE_BITS :: pub const COARSE_AGE_BITS: u32 = 16; :: The width of a coarse Age, in bits: the fine age scaled into a 16-bit address space (coarse = fine >> (AGE_BITS − COARSE_AGE_BITS)). One coarse tick is 420 / 65536 s ≈ 6.41 ms. :: doc comment
item+ const src/epoch.rs :: COARSE_AGE_MAX :: pub const COARSE_AGE_MAX: u64 = COARSE_AGES_PER_EPOCH - 1; :: The largest coarse Age: the final coarse step of an epoch. :: doc comment
item+ function src/epoch.rs :: age_at :: pub fn age_at(unix_seconds: i64) -> Option<u64> { :: ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- @fn age_at  the Age of the given unix timestamp: its nominal position within its epoch, `floor(seconds_into_epoch × 2^42 / 420)`, in the range 0..=AGE_MAX. `None` before the anchor, exactly as `seconds_into_epoch` — a pre-epoch instant is inside no epoch, so it has no age. Whole-second input fixes the age at that second's first instant; `age_at_nanos` refines it with the sub-second part.  An age is a *local* position: 1 ms of clock skew moves it by ≈ 10.47 million ages, so ages order events on one clock — the epoch count is the coordinate that travels between machines. ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- :: doc comment
item+ function src/epoch.rs :: age_at_nanos :: pub fn age_at_nanos(unix_seconds: i64, nanoseconds: u32) -> Option<u64> { :: ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- @fn age_at_nanos  the Age of a timestamp given with sub-second precision: `unix_seconds` plus `nanoseconds` (0..1_000_000_000) within that second. The age is `floor(elapsed_ns × 2^42 / (420 × 10^9))` over the nanoseconds elapsed in the containing epoch, computed in 128-bit arithmetic (elapsed_ns × 2^42 overflows u64). `None` before the anchor, and `None` for an out-of-range nanosecond part. With `nanoseconds == 0` the result agrees exactly with `age_at`. ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- :: doc comment
item+ function src/epoch.rs :: age_start :: pub fn age_start(epoch: u64, age: u64) -> Option<i64> { :: ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- @fn age_start  the unix timestamp of the whole second in which the given age of the given epoch begins: `epoch_start(epoch) + floor(age × 420 / 2^42)`. `None` for EE 0, for `age > AGE_MAX`, and when that second is not representable as an i64 unix timestamp.  This is the coarsest faithful inverse of `age_at`, and its coarseness is the module's honesty about resolution: an age is ≈ 95.5 ps wide, so ~10.47 billion ages begin inside each second and no whole-second timestamp can name one age's exact start. The inverse therefore names the containing second — expect it to read one second early when the age in force at a second boundary began just before it. ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- :: doc comment
item+ function src/epoch.rs :: coarse_age_at :: pub fn coarse_age_at(unix_seconds: i64) -> Option<u64> { :: ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- @fn coarse_age_at  the coarse Age of the given unix timestamp: the fine age scaled into a 16-bit address space, `age_at(t) >> 26`, equivalently `floor(seconds_into_epoch × 65536 / 420)`, in the range 0..=COARSE_AGE_MAX. `None` before the anchor, exactly as `age_at`.  This is the register-grade form. One coarse tick is ≈ 6.41 ms, so where a fine age (≈ 95.5 ps) is destroyed by any real clock skew — 1 ms is ≈ 10.47 million fine ages — NTP-class skew of a few milliseconds costs a coarse age about a tick. Coarse ages are therefore approximately comparable across machines, and they are the Age component the register system carries. ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- :: doc comment
