item~ function src/epoch.rs :: age_at :: pub fn age_at(unix_seconds: i64) -> Option<u64> { :: ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- @fn age_at  the Sentron Age of the given unix timestamp: its nominal position within its epoch, `floor(seconds_into_epoch × 2^42 / 420)`, in the range 0..=AGE_MAX. `None` before the anchor, exactly as `seconds_into_epoch` — a pre-epoch instant is inside no epoch, so it has no Sentron Age. Whole-second input fixes the Sentron Age at that second's first instant; `age_at_nanos` refines it with the sub-second part.  A Sentron Age is a *local* position: 1 ms of clock skew moves it by ≈ 10.47 million Sentron Ages, so Sentron Ages order events on one clock — the epoch count is the coordinate that travels between machines. ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- :: doc comment
item~ function src/epoch.rs :: age_at_nanos :: pub fn age_at_nanos(unix_seconds: i64, nanoseconds: u32) -> Option<u64> { :: ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- @fn age_at_nanos  the Sentron Age of a timestamp given with sub-second precision: `unix_seconds` plus `nanoseconds` (0..1_000_000_000) within that second. The Sentron Age is `floor(elapsed_ns × 2^42 / (420 × 10^9))` over the nanoseconds elapsed in the containing epoch, computed in 128-bit arithmetic (elapsed_ns × 2^42 overflows u64). `None` before the anchor, and `None` for an out-of-range nanosecond part. With `nanoseconds == 0` the result agrees exactly with `age_at`. ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- :: doc comment
item~ function src/epoch.rs :: age_start :: pub fn age_start(epoch: u64, age: u64) -> Option<i64> { :: ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- @fn age_start  the unix timestamp of the whole second in which the given Sentron Age of the given epoch begins: `epoch_start(epoch) + floor(age × 420 / 2^42)`. `None` for EE 0, for `age > AGE_MAX`, and when that second is not representable as an i64 unix timestamp.  This is the coarsest faithful inverse of `age_at`, and its coarseness is the module's honesty about resolution: a Sentron Age is ≈ 95.5 ps wide, so ~10.47 billion Sentron Ages begin inside each second and no whole-second timestamp can name one Sentron Age's exact start. The inverse therefore names the containing second — expect it to read one second early when the Sentron Age in force at a second boundary began just before it. ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- :: doc comment
item~ function src/epoch.rs :: coarse_age_at :: pub fn coarse_age_at(unix_seconds: i64) -> Option<u64> { :: ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- @fn coarse_age_at  the coarse Sentron Age of the given unix timestamp: the fine Sentron Age scaled into a 16-bit address space, `age_at(t) >> 26`, equivalently `floor(seconds_into_epoch × 65536 / 420)`, in the range 0..=COARSE_AGE_MAX. `None` before the anchor, exactly as `age_at`.  This is the register-grade form. One coarse tick is ≈ 6.41 ms, so where a fine Sentron Age (≈ 95.5 ps) is destroyed by any real clock skew — 1 ms is ≈ 10.47 million fine Sentron Ages — NTP-class skew of a few milliseconds costs a coarse Sentron Age about a tick. Coarse Sentron Ages are therefore approximately comparable across machines, and they are the Age component the register system carries. ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- :: doc comment
